Fimages%2F42bd884e-40d8-4339-aea4-9c2c16f090d4_1599x899.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class="image-caption"><em>(Graphic by Paul)</em></figcaption></figure></div><p><em><strong>Note: </strong>This post is not paywalled. Enjoy! &#8212; Paul</em></p><div><hr></div><p>Earlier this week I wrote about an <a href="https://www.inconspicuous.info/p/the-power-of-the-unlikely-time-capsule">old receipt</a> that my grandmother saved. Today I want to follow up with another story related to her &#8212; one that I&#8217;m pretty certain many of you can relate to.</p><p>Here&#8217;s the deal: Shortly before my father died in 2009, he gave me his childhood stamp collection. He had kept the collection in an album that his parents gave to him as a Chanukah present in 1935, when he was 11 years old. I know that&#8217;s when they gave it to him because of this inscription on the first page:</p><div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg" width="724.53125" height="531.9532323145604" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:1069,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:724.53125,&quot;bytes&quot;:1341707,&quot;alt&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://www.inconspicuous.info/i/203164862?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:&quot;center&quot;,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-normal" alt="" title="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!GySy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F890d4d0e-8c6b-4330-bfae-b13031d42b88_2569x1886.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class="image-caption"><em>Someone had very nice handwriting. (Photo by Paul)</em></figcaption></figure></div><p>Yes, my father&#8217;s name was Irwin. But I&#8217;m more interested in the other two names: &#8220;Dad&#8221; and, especially, &#8220;Mommy.&#8221;</p><p>My paternal grandparents&#8217; names were Charles and Fannie, but of course I never knew them by those names. In fact, I didn&#8217;t know my grandfather at all &#8212; he died in 1966, when I was a toddler (and my other grandfather died long before I was born). But my grandmother &#8212; the same one who saved the refrigerator receipt &#8212; was a big part of my life until her death when I was 16, and I knew her as Nanny. That&#8217;s what my parents always called her, and that&#8217;s how my mom still refers to her when she comes up in conversation. I&#8217;ve always known that she was my father&#8217;s mother, of course, but I still find it a bit jarring to see that she signed the stamp album as &#8220;Mommy,&#8221; because I never knew her in that role.</p><p>And that&#8217;t the thing: &#8220;Mommy&#8221; and &#8220;Nanny&#8221; aren&#8217;t just names, they&#8217;re <em>roles, </em>and grandparents end up playing both of them.</p><p>My other grandmother &#8212; my mom&#8217;s mom &#8212; went by Nana. So we had Nanny and Nana, which could occasionally be confusing when they were both in the same room. I recently asked my mother how these two nicknames were chosen. She said she wasn&#8217;t sure, but she thinks my grandparents were consulted about what they wanted to be called, and those were the names they chose.</p><p>My two brothers and I all opted not to have kids of our own, so we never had to address the issue of generational nicknames. Or to put it another way, I&#8217;m very ignorant on this topic. So here are some questions (feel free to post your responses in the comments):</p><ul><li><p>If your grandparents were alive when you were growing up, what did you call them? Do you know how those names were chosen?</p></li><li><p>If you have kids, what do they call their grandparents (i.e., your parents and in-laws), and how were those names arrived at?</p></li><li><p>If you are a grandparent, what do your grandchildren call you, how was that name chosen, and how do you feel about it? Also, does it ever feel weird for your nickname to toggle back and forth (between &#8220;Dad&#8221; and Grandpa,&#8221; say), depending on which generation you&#8217;re interacting with?</p></li><li><p>If you think you&#8217;ll probably become a grandparent, have you thought about what you&#8217;d like to be called?</p></li><li><p>Any related issues worth addressing?</p></li></ul><p>Again, I realize this is probably basic stuff for most families, but not so much for mine. Thanks in advance for educating me on this topic!</p><div class="subscription-widget-wrap-editor" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.inconspicuous.info/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe&quot;,&quot;language&quot;:&quot;en&quot;}" data-component-name="SubscribeWidgetToDOM"><div class="subscription-widget show-subscribe"><div class="preamble"><p class="cta-caption">Inconspicuous Consumption is 100% reader-supported. Please do your part by becoming a paid subscriber. Thanks!</p></div><form class="subscription-widget-subscribe"><input type="email" class="email-input" name="email" placeholder="Type your email&#8230;" tabindex="-1"><input type="submit" class="button primary" value="Subscribe"><div class="fake-input-wrapper"><div class="fake-input"></div><div class="fake-button"></div></div></form></div></div><p><em>Paul Lukas has been obsessing over the inconspicuous for most of his life, and has been writing about those obsessions for more than 30 years. You can <a href="mailto:plukas64@gmail.com">contact him here</a>.</em></p><p></p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[The Power of the Unlikely Time Capsule]]></title><description><![CDATA[As I recently discovered, even the most random-seeming artifact can unlock a series of potent memories.]]></description><link>https://www.inconspicuous.info/p/the-power-of-the-unlikely-time-capsule</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.inconspicuous.info/p/the-power-of-the-unlikely-time-capsule</guid><pubDate>Tue, 23 Jun 2026 12:02:02 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/6b05700b-6830-4ffa-ad23-fe406f35382f_1958x1292.png" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_2400,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg" width="1200" height="661.8131868131868" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/cebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;large&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:803,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:1200,&quot;bytes&quot;:469746,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:&quot;https://www.inconspicuous.info/i/202897648?img=https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg&quot;,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:&quot;center&quot;,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" class="sizing-large" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!u5XR!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fcebdc58e-bb65-42bd-aa48-2ee4aa661bc8_2198x1212.jpeg 1456w" sizes="100vw" fetchpriority="high"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class="image-caption"><em>(Photo by Paul)</em></figcaption></figure></div><p>I was recently helping my mom clean out a dresser drawer, which was filled with jewelry she no longer wears and other detritus. At the back of the drawer was an unremarkable-looking women&#8217;s wallet, which Mom said she didn&#8217;t recognize. It had a few zippered compartments, one of which contained the slip of paper shown above &#8212; a receipt for a two-door Hotpoint refrigerator delivered to a Mrs. Lewkowitz in the Long Island town of Patchogue on August 7th, 1975.</p><p>Mrs. Lewkowitz was my grandmother &#8212; my father&#8217;s mother<a class="footnote-anchor" data-component-name="FootnoteAnchorToDOM" id="footnote-anchor-1" href="#footnote-1" target="_self">1</a> &#8212; and Patchogue Gardens was the apartment complex where she lived when I was growing up, so the wallet must have belonged to her. She died in the fall of 1980, five years after the refrigerator delivery. My parents must have thought that my mom might one day use the wallet, so they apparently stowed it in the back of the drawer with the jewelry and other accessories, where it remained for another 46 years. During that time, my parents moved to a retirement community (2004), my father died (2009), my mom moved to a smaller retirement apartment (2010), and then she moved again to assisted living (2015). Each time, the dresser moved with her; each time, the wallet was inside the dresser drawer and the fridge receipt was inside the wallet, waiting to be discovered.</p>
