165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class="image-caption"><em>Tracy, California. (Photos by Flickr users Robby Virus [left] and Frank Kelsey)</em></figcaption></figure></div><p>Shortly after I published Tuesday&#8217;s deep dive about <a href="https://www.inconspicuous.info/p/where-should-the-apostrophe-go-on">apostrophe positioning on vertically lettered signs</a>, I became aware of a particularly interesting example: Ralph&#8217;s Club, located in Tracy, California. As you can see in the photos above, the sign includes a centered apostrophe, but it&#8217;s turned sideways, so it looks more like a hyphen! I&#8217;ve never seen anything quite like that before.</p><p>Those photos are are both from the early 2010s. The sign was refurbished in 2023. The bad news is that they used LEDs instead of true neon (grrrr), plus they monkeyed with some of the letterforms (the new &#8220;A&#8221; is particularly awful). But the good news is that they retained the odd apostrophe:</p><div class="captioned-image-container"><figure><a class="image-link image2 is-viewable-img" target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!j-Vj!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe5447480-4f6d-4d87-ba4a-a0331144749b_960x1280.jpeg" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div class="image2-inset"><picture><sour